FreeBSD-SA-26:85.kqueue                                     Security Advisory
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          Multiple vulnerabilities in kevent(2)

Category:       core
Module:         kqueue
Announced:      2026-11-17
Credits:        OpenAI Security Research Team
Credits:        Dennys Kaluzny Pereira
Affects:        All supported versions of FreeBSD.
Corrected:      2026-11-17 17:06:05 UTC (stable/15, 15.2-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:08:22 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p7)
                2026-11-17 17:02:50 UTC (stable/14, 14.5-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:05:13 UTC (releng/14.5, 14.5-RELEASE-p4)
                2026-11-17 17:04:14 UTC (releng/14.4, 14.4-RELEASE-p13)
CVE Name:       CVE-2026-101320, CVE-2026-127750

For general information regarding FreeBSD Security Advisories,
including descriptions of the fields above, security branches, and the
following sections, please visit <URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

The kqueue(2) and kevent(2) system calls provide a generic mechanism for
notifying a process of kernel events, such as file descriptor activity,
signals and timer expirations.  On FreeBSD 15 and later, a kqueue created
by kqueuex(2) with the KQUEUE_CPONFORK flag is copied, along with its
registered events, into the child process by fork(2).

II.  Problem Description

When an EVFILT_TIMER event was removed, the kernel failed to wait for a
concurrently firing timer callback if the owning process had been stopped
and continued by signals.  The callback could then run after the event's
memory was freed.  (CVE-2026-101320)

When copying a KQUEUE_CPONFORK kqueue during fork(2), the kernel
temporarily dropped the kqueue lock while iterating over its lists of
events.  If another thread registered an event in the meantime, the array
holding these lists could be reallocated, and the copy loop would then
modify the freed array.  This bug does not affect FreeBSD 14.
(CVE-2026-127750)

III. Impact

An unprivileged local user can exploit the EVFILT_TIMER race to obtain
superuser privileges.  The KQUEUE_CPONFORK race also corrupts kernel
memory and may likewise be exploitable.

IV.  Workaround

No workaround is available.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your vulnerable system to a supported FreeBSD stable or
release / security branch (releng) dated after the correction date,
and reboot the system.

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your vulnerable system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for a security update"

2) To update your vulnerable system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for a security update"

3) To update your vulnerable system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

[FreeBSD 15.x]
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:85/kqueue-15.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:85/kqueue-15.patch.asc
# gpg --verify kqueue-15.patch.asc

[FreeBSD 14.x]
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:85/kqueue-14.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:85/kqueue-14.patch.asc
# gpg --verify kqueue-14.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile your kernel as described in
<URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/kernelconfig.html> and reboot the
system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
stable/15/                              ac7d692a4455    stable/15-n286876
releng/15.1/                            701478cfd697  releng/15.1-n283679
stable/14/                              65b1a6ca6095    stable/14-n275579
releng/14.5/                            6afa595f1f16  releng/14.5-n274914
releng/14.4/                            9823ecaea3c0  releng/14.4-n273814
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the following command to see which files were modified by a
particular commit:

# git show --stat <commit hash>

Or visit the following URL, replacing NNNNNN with the hash:

<URL:https://cgit.freebsd.org/src/commit/?id=NNNNNN>

To determine the commit count in a working tree (for comparison against
nNNNNNN in the table above), run:

# git rev-list --count --first-parent HEAD

